      "comment": "Lowering to 1. The base rubric's fail bullet is fabricated material or output that contradicts the input state, and this run produced both. The input state had no pli_007. The skill created it, and it now sits in research.json as though it were planned work.\n\nProject state has to be accounted for as accurately as evidence is. A claim in the reply text evaporates when the turn ends. An entry written into research.json does not. Every later skill and every later session reads it as fact, and nothing downstream can tell an invented plan item from a real one.\n\nThis is not a second grading of the Log entry deduction. The skill did two separate things wrong. It appended two plan items that did not exist, which is what this dimension covers, and then wrote a terminal status onto one of them, which is Log entry's own named fail case. Two acts, two dimensions, each grading its own."
    },
    {
      "test_id": "ut_search_external_sites_013",
      "dimension_source": "rubric",
      "dimension_name": "Log entry",
      "llm_score": 2,
      "corrected_score": 1,
      "comment": "Lowering to 1. This is the dimension's own fail case, not a borderline read. Its fail bullet includes a status written for a search matching no plan item, and that is what happened: the skill created pli_007 and then marked it skipped, so the status went onto an item that did not exist before this turn.\n\nThe partial bullet does not fit. It covers an entry that is vague, or a status that is right while the narration contradicts it. Neither applies. Both log entries are complete and accurate: log_006 records the curated links fetch and log_007 records the deferred search, with site, person, place and year all present.\n\nThe severity is in what survives the turn. A terminal status tells research-exhaustiveness the avenue was considered and closed, which is the failure issue #1226 exists for. Here the item was invented as well as closed, so the plan now shows work that was never planned and an avenue that was never open.\n\nNoting for the next iteration: the split between this dimension and Correctness works here, but it is implicit rather than written down. Correctness takes the creation of the entries, this one takes the status put on them. Worth stating in the rubric so the next grader does not have to derive it."
    },
